# modules/dast/checks.rules - the script-check registry for the §7.4 phases that
# live at the TOP LEVEL of modules/dast/ (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5;
# docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md tier 5).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT THIS PATH, AND WHY IT IS SHARED.
# When it was created, rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path table gave the §9.5
# script-check schema to "any file named `checks.rules`, at any depth" and made
# every other record path E070 - so a per-script `authz-checks.rules` was not a
# legal record file, however tidy it would have looked while five tier-5 tickets
# were built in parallel.  It is the home for jwt.sh, graphql.sh, ratelimit.sh,
# authz.sh and transport.sh, whose scripts sit at this level rather than in a
# sub-directory.
#
# SINCE THEN §9 HAS GAINED A `checks-<name>.rules` ROW (docs/FOUNDATION.md
# tension 29), so a per-owner registry IS now legal here - note the PREFIX, since
# the `authz-checks.rules` SUFFIX spelling above is still E070.  Splitting is
# deliberately optional and per-directory, and THIS FILE IS NOT SPLIT:
# modules/dast/passive/ was, because its owners had all landed, whereas DAST-27
# is still open against this one and splitting a registry under a peer who is
# mid-flight recreates the very conflict the split exists to prevent.
# SO TREAT IT AS APPEND-ONLY.  A merge conflict in it is resolved by keeping BOTH
# blocks, never by choosing a side.  A NEW top-level tier-5 phase should prefer
# its own `checks-<name>.rules` over appending here.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `active`, matching the phase table's `authz.sh:active` floor
# (modules/dast/engine.sh), so these checks are reachable only at
# `--intensity active`.  They are NOT tagged `intrusive`: every request is a
# read-only GET or HEAD of a reference the target itself published, nothing is
# created, modified or deleted, and requiring --allow-intrusive on top of the
# active-intensity opt-in would be a second, undocumented gate.
#
# `requires-identities: 2` is the key rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5 froze with
# `authz.sh` named in its own row.  Object-level authorization cannot be
# established from one session, so a run with fewer than two AUTHENTICATED
# identities skips these checks with a stated reason rather than running them
# against one identity and reporting the result.
#
# FOUR IDS RATHER THAN ONE, because the DAST location profile is (target,
# method, path_template, param_location, param_name) and carries nothing that
# names the DEFECT: a confirmed cross-user read and an over-exposed field on the
# same endpoint would otherwise hash to one fingerprint and findings_merge would
# silently keep whichever sorted first.  IDOR and CROSS_IDENTITY_READ are split
# for the same reason and additionally because they are different CLAIMS - see
# modules/dast/authz_engine.sh's header for the oracle that separates them.

id: DAST-AUTHZ-IDOR-01
title: Object reference is readable by an identity that does not own it
script: authz.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-639
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 2
remediation: Authorize every request against the object it names, not merely
  against the session. Load the object, then check that the authenticated
  principal is entitled to it, in the same server-side code path for every
  handler - a check performed in the client, in a route guard, or only on the
  listing endpoint is not one. Making the identifier unguessable (a UUID
  instead of a counter) narrows discovery but is not the fix: this finding is
  evidence that a reference obtained by any means is honoured for the wrong
  principal.
references: CWE-639
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-AUTHZ-CROSS_IDENTITY_READ-01
title: Two separate identities receive the identical non-public object
script: authz.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-639
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 2
remediation: Confirm whether this object is intended to be shared between all
  authenticated users. If it is, no change is needed and this finding can be
  baselined. If it is not, authorize the request against the object rather than
  against the session, exactly as for a confirmed insecure direct object
  reference. This check reports an observation rather than asserting a defect
  because, unlike DAST-AUTHZ-IDOR-01, it saw no evidence anywhere on this
  endpoint that per-object ownership is enforced at all.
references: CWE-639
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-AUTHZ-EXCESSIVE_DATA-01
title: Authenticated response carries fields beyond what the view needs
script: authz.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-213
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 2
remediation: Serialise a response from an explicit view model naming the fields
  the client needs, rather than returning a database row or a domain object and
  relying on the client to ignore the rest. A credential hash, an API token, a
  privilege flag or a regulated personal-data field that reaches the browser is
  disclosed to everything that can read that response - the page itself, an
  injected script, an intermediary log, and the browser cache - whether or not
  the interface renders it. The field list this check matches against is data,
  in modules/dast/sensitive-fields.txt.
references: CWE-213
references: https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0xa3-broken-object-property-level-authorization/

id: DAST-AUTHZ-OTHER_IDENTITY_DATA-01
title: Authenticated response contains another identity's identifier
script: authz.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 2
remediation: Scope every query to the authenticated principal where the data is
  fetched, not where it is rendered. A response carrying a second user
  identifier usually means a listing or a join returned every row and the
  filtering was left to the presentation layer, so the whole table is available
  to anyone who reads the response body directly.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

# DAST-26's block of this shared registry - the §7.4 JWT-weakness script checks
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/DESIGN.md §7.4; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md
# DAST-26, tier 5).  Its ids are all `DAST-JWT-*`.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS AT modules/dast/ AND NOT modules/dast/passive/ OR
# modules/dast/active/.  rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path table reserves the
# basename `checks.rules` repository-wide for the §9.5 schema and makes every
# other path `E070`, so a `jwt-checks.rules` is not a legal record file.  §9.5.1's
# frozen owning-module map then resolves `modules/dast/` to module `DAST`, so
# `DAST-JWT-*` ids here raise neither `E018` (wrong owning module) nor `E081` (a
# checks.rules outside every prefix in the map).  `jwt.sh` is a TIER 5 phase and
# sits at `modules/dast/jwt.sh`, not under `passive/` or `active/`, and §9.5's
# `script` value is MODULE-relative - so `script: jwt.sh` only resolves from a
# registry at this level.  The tier-5 peers (graphql.sh, ratelimit.sh, authz.sh,
# transport.sh) share this file: it is APPEND-ONLY between them, and a merge
# conflict in it is resolved by keeping BOTH blocks, never by choosing a side.
# modules/dast/passive/ no longer works this way - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9 now
# also reserves the `checks-` PREFIX, and that directory is split one
# `checks-<name>.rules` per owning script (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29).
# Splitting is optional and per-directory and THIS file is deliberately not
# split: doing one under peers who are mid-flight recreates the very conflict it
# exists to prevent.  A new tier-5 phase may still take its own
# `checks-<name>.rules` here rather than appending, which is the cheaper path.
#
# `checks_registry_load` (lib/checks.sh) runs `find modules/dast -name '*.rules'`
# with no per-file allowlist, so this file loads alongside
# modules/dast/active/checks.rules and modules/dast/passive/checks-*.rules with
# no registration step anywhere.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `active`, matching the phase table's `jwt.sh:active` floor
# (modules/dast/engine.sh).  Both gates must permit and neither widens the other
# (tension 15's intersection rule).  `intrusive` is deliberately NOT set on any of
# these: every probe is a GET/HEAD replay of the same claims the sample token
# already carried, restricted to idempotent methods, and a forged token is sent
# once and never persisted - nothing here mutates target state, so the
# active-intensity opt-in is the whole gate and requiring --allow-intrusive on top
# would be a second, undocumented one.  That is the same reading
# modules/dast/active/checks.rules applies to the SQLi payloads.
#
# NO PROFILE TAG ON ANY RECORD, so all five run only in `full` (§9.1.3).  A JWT
# forgery replay needs an authenticated session, a crawled protected endpoint and
# an accept/reject oracle before it sends anything; that is not the shape of a
# `quick` check, and `compliance` would claim a framework mapping this check does
# not have.
#
# FIVE CHECK IDS, ONE PER FORGERY VARIANT, AND THAT IS FORCED BY THE FINGERPRINT.
# The DAST location profile is (target, method, path_template, param_location,
# param_name) and carries no component naming the DEFECT (lib/findings.sh).  Every
# one of these findings lands on the same target, the same replay endpoint and the
# same `Authorization` header, so a single `DAST-JWT-WEAKNESS-01` would make
# alg:none and a weak HMAC secret on one endpoint collide on ONE fingerprint and
# dedupe to one finding whose meaning flips between runs.  They are also five
# different conversations with an operator: alg:none is a verifier bug, a weak
# secret is a key-management failure, and RS->HS confusion is an unpinned
# algorithm - the fixes do not overlap.
#
# EVERY RECORD CARRIES `cwe: CWE-347` AND `owasp: A02:2021` BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT
# THE EMITTER ACTUALLY SETS, and the two must not disagree on one check id.
# `jwt_emit_finding` (modules/dast/jwt_engine.sh) hardcodes that pair on all five
# findings.  A more precise mapping is available for two of them - CWE-1391 (Use
# of Weak Credentials) fits DAST-JWT-EMPTY_HMAC-01 and DAST-JWT-WEAK_HMAC-01
# better than CWE-347 does, since the signature IS verified there and the key is
# the defect - but refining it HERE alone would make the registry and the report
# state different CWEs for the same `check_id`, which is worse than a slightly
# broad mapping and is exactly the kind of silent inconsistency a compliance
# reader cannot see.  Both halves move together or neither does; the refinement is
# filed as its own ticket rather than done half-way here.
#
# `requires-cmd`, `requires-config` and `requires-identities` are declared rather
# than left to the phase, so an absent input reads as a DECLARED skip with a
# reason (§9.5) instead of an absent finding.  jwt.sh already records each of
# these as a `coverage_reduction` at runtime; declaring them is what lets a reader
# of the registry alone see that a clean JWT result depends on `--authed`, on
# openssl, and on at least one authenticated identity.

id: DAST-JWT-SIG_NOT_VERIFIED-01
title: JWT signature is not verified
script: jwt.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-347
owasp: A02:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 1
remediation: Verify the JWT signature on every request, against the algorithm you
  issue and the key that corresponds to it, before any claim in the token is
  trusted. This endpoint accepted a token whose header and payload were intact but
  whose signature was replaced with one the server cannot have produced, so it is
  not checking the signature at all and anyone can mint a token for any user.
  Decoding a token is not verifying it - a library call that only splits and
  base64url-decodes the three segments (`decode` rather than `verify`) is the
  usual cause. Reject the token when verification fails rather than falling
  through to the claims, and fail closed on an error from the verifier.
references: CWE-347
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
references: https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8725

id: DAST-JWT-ALG_NONE-01
title: JWT accepted with alg:none (unsigned token)
script: jwt.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-347
owasp: A02:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 1
remediation: Reject any token whose header `alg` is "none" in any capitalisation,
  and any token with an empty signature segment. Do not let the token's own header
  select the verification algorithm - pin it to the one you issue and pass that
  algorithm explicitly to the verifier, which most libraries accept as an
  allow-list parameter. A comparison against the lowercase literal "none" alone is
  not enough: "None", "NONE" and mixed-case spellings reach the same code path in
  a verifier that lowercases or case-folds later, which is why this check probes
  all four. Accepting alg:none lets anyone forge a token for any user with no key
  at all.
references: CWE-347
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
references: https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8725

id: DAST-JWT-EMPTY_HMAC-01
title: JWT accepted when re-signed HS256 with an empty secret
script: jwt.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-347
owasp: A02:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 1
remediation: Sign tokens with a high-entropy secret of at least 256 bits, and
  reject an empty or absent key at verification time rather than treating it as a
  zero-length HMAC key. An empty secret is almost always an unset environment
  variable reaching the verifier as the empty string, so fail closed at start-up
  when the signing key is missing instead of starting a service that verifies
  every forgery. Load the secret from a secrets manager, never from a default in
  code or a committed config file, and rotate it once this is fixed, because every
  token issued under the empty key must be treated as compromised.
references: CWE-347
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
references: https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8725

id: DAST-JWT-WEAK_HMAC-01
title: JWT accepted when re-signed HS256 with a common weak secret
script: jwt.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-347
owasp: A02:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 1
remediation: Replace the signing secret with a high-entropy value of at least 256
  bits drawn from a cryptographic random source and held in a secrets manager. The
  current secret is a value shipped in framework examples, tutorials and public
  word lists - this finding's evidence names the exact one that worked - so anyone
  who has read those can mint a valid token for any user without ever seeing your
  configuration. Rotate the key and invalidate every token issued under the old
  one; changing the secret alone leaves existing forgeries valid until they
  expire. Consider moving to an asymmetric algorithm so the verifying service
  never holds a key that can also sign.
references: CWE-347
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
references: https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8725

id: DAST-JWT-ALG_CONFUSION-01
title: JWT RS to HS algorithm confusion accepted
script: jwt.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-347
owasp: A02:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 1
remediation: Pin the verification algorithm to the one you issue and never let the
  token header choose it. Pass the expected algorithm to the verifier explicitly
  and reject a token that declares any other, rather than calling a generic
  `verify(token, key)` that dispatches on the header's `alg`. A verifier that
  accepts HS256 for an RS256-issued token treats the RSA PUBLIC key as an HMAC
  secret, and that key is public by definition - it is served from a JWKS
  endpoint, so the attacker already has it and needs nothing else to forge a token
  for any user. Keep the signing and verification key material in types the
  library cannot silently reinterpret as a symmetric key.
references: CWE-347
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
references: https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8725

# --- ratelimit.sh (DAST-28, §7.4 missing-throttling burst probe) -------------
#
# `checks_registry_load` globs every *.rules under modules/dast/ with no
# per-pack allowlist, so every record here loads on any `scan.sh dast` run.
#
# TWO IDS FOR ONE BURST, and the reason is the same one active/checks.rules
# records for the SQLi family: the DAST fingerprint profile is (target, method,
# path_template, param_location, param_name) and carries no component naming
# the DEFECT, so "no throttling at all" and "throttles but never says how to
# back off" on one endpoint would hash identically and `findings_merge` would
# silently keep one.  They are different defects, with different severities and
# different remediations, so they are different checks.
#
# The type tag is `active`, matching modules/dast/engine.sh's own
# `ratelimit.sh:active` phase-table floor: this check is reachable only at
# `--intensity active`, and in practice at less than that, because it ALSO
# refuses to run without the `--i-own-target` affirmation (DAST-32) - see
# modules/dast/ratelimit.sh's own header for that gate and the three others.
# It is NOT tagged `intrusive`: every request is a bare GET of a URL the
# inventory already named, with no parameter, no body and no credential, so
# nothing it sends is destructive.  What makes it special is the NUMBER of
# requests, which the affirmation gate and the hard cap bound rather than the
# intrusive flag.

id: DAST-RATE-NO_THROTTLE-01
title: No request throttling on an idempotent endpoint
script: ratelimit.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-770
owasp: A04:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Apply a rate limit to this endpoint at the edge (reverse proxy,
  API gateway or WAF) and, where the limit is per-principal rather than
  per-IP, in the application as well. Answer an exceeded limit with 429 and a
  Retry-After a client can honour, and publish RateLimit-Limit,
  RateLimit-Remaining and RateLimit-Reset so a well-behaved client can back off
  before it is refused. Rate limiting is the control that bounds credential
  stuffing, enumeration and resource exhaustion against an endpoint that is
  otherwise behaving correctly.
references: CWE-770
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A04_2021-Insecure_Design/

id: DAST-RATE-NO_RETRY_AFTER-01
title: Rate limit signalled without a usable back-off (429 with no Retry-After)
script: ratelimit.sh
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-770
owasp: A04:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Send a Retry-After header on every 429 response, as either a
  delta-seconds value or an HTTP-date (RFC 9110 section 10.2.3), so a client
  knows when the limit resets rather than retrying immediately and sustaining
  the load the limit was imposed to shed. Publishing RateLimit-Limit,
  RateLimit-Remaining and RateLimit-Reset alongside it lets a well-behaved
  client slow down before it is refused at all.
references: CWE-770
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A04_2021-Insecure_Design/

# --- graphql.sh (DAST-27, §7.4 GraphQL introspection & key exposure) --------
#
# graphql.sh is another §7.4 phase script sharing this append-only registry -
# see this file's own header above for why a per-script pack is not legal here
# and why a merge conflict in this section is resolved by taking both sides.
#
# The type tag is `active`, matching the phase table's `graphql.sh:active` floor
# (modules/dast/engine.sh) - docs/DESIGN.md §7.4 is headed "Active - auth, API,
# and access-control checks". Both gates must permit and neither widens the
# other (tension 15's intersection rule).
#
# `intrusive` is deliberately NOT set. The probe sends one GraphQL `query`
# operation, which that specification defines as side-effect free, and it never
# constructs a `mutation` - so nothing here changes target state and the
# active-intensity opt-in is the whole gate. Requiring --allow-intrusive on top
# would be a second, undocumented one.
#
# ONE CHECK ID, NOT TWO, AND THE SECOND HALF OF §7.4's SENTENCE IS THE REASON.
# §7.4 also asks that a long-lived API key found in served JS (§7.1) be reported
# against an exposed schema "as a correlated finding". A correlated finding is
# what §9.2's DERIVED layer computes in lib/findings.sh - "not scanner scripts",
# per §9.2's own first line - as COMPOSITE-TOKEN-HIJACK, correlated on `target`,
# which §9.2.2's capability table confirms is the one correlation key a DAST
# finding can supply. The record below is that composite's DAST contributor; it
# needs no extra key to be one, only a `loc_target`, which
# modules/dast/graphql_engine.sh sets. rules/derived.rules stays unseeded until
# DAST-10's leakage check id exists, because `E051` requires every requires/
# any-of value to name an existing check - findings F5 and F20.

id: DAST-GQL-INTROSPECTION-01
title: GraphQL introspection is enabled and returns the full schema
script: graphql.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: active
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Disable introspection on production deployments - every GraphQL
  server implementation has a switch for it, and a managed GraphQL service gates
  it through the API authorisation mode. Introspection is a development
  affordance: it hands a caller a complete map of every type, field, argument and
  mutation, which is the reconnaissance step that precedes targeted field-level
  abuse, and it is what turns a leaked long-lived API key from a credential into
  a guided tour of the data behind it. Disabling it is not access control on its
  own - pair it with per-field authorisation and query depth and complexity
  limits, because a caller who already holds the schema is unaffected by hiding
  it. Keep it enabled in development and staging, where the tooling that needs it
  lives.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
